The '''Act on Free Movement of 1838''' was a Sartan parliamentary reform that abolished serfdom and allowed peasants to move more freely between estates, towns, and mining districts. The law compensated the lords for the loss of serf labour, but the payments placed a heavy burden on the royal finances.

== Background ==

By the late 1830s, Sarta was experiencing increased movement from the countryside to the towns. Growing administrative demand, mining activity, and urban labour markets drew many peasants away from rural estates. At the same time, peasant unrest continued in areas where serfdom remained in force, especially on large inland holdings.

The parliamentary order that had emerged after the [[Second Sartan Civil War]] was dominated by lords, but even within that framework there was growing recognition that serfdom was becoming difficult to sustain. Reformers argued that freer movement would reduce unrest and better match the kingdom's changing economy, while conservatives insisted that the nobility had to be compensated for any loss of labour obligations.

== Terms ==

The act ended serfdom in Sarta and granted former serfs the right to move without the direct legal restrictions that had tied them to particular estates. The law also required compensation to landowning lords, which preserved elite support for the reform but left the treasury short of funds.

Because the state paid for the reform rather than simply abolishing the old obligations, the act weakened royal finances at a time when the kingdom was already managing the costs of administration and territorial negotiation. Even so, the reform proved politically durable and became one of the defining legal changes of the period.

== Significance ==

The Act on Free Movement accelerated urbanisation and labour migration, especially toward [[Leona]], the coastal towns, and the mining districts of the [[Sierra Madre]]. It also reduced the legal basis of peasant rebellion in the serfdoms, although rural unrest did not disappear entirely.

In political memory, the act was often treated as a pragmatic compromise: it weakened the old estate system without provoking a broader collapse of the parliamentary order. Its cost, however, remained a point of criticism for later commentators who argued that the compensation payments drained the treasury too sharply.

